h must have been the most important, given how it was used to calque Juppiter, akin to Sanskrit अग्नि (agni), Hindu god of fire. This is endorsed by Orel (1998). Oštir (1925) tentatively connects to Etruscan 𐌀𐌉𐌔 (ais, “god”) or to 𐌖𐌍𐌉 (uni, “Juno”). Mjeda (1932) derives it from Ancient Greek ἕνη (hénē), comparing its use in t…","tpl":[{"k":76,"lang":"sq"}]},"rel":{"d":["Enjte"]}},{"w":"Enjte","p":"PROPN","s":[{"g":"Zeus","t":177,"x":["Eδe ḱŭeitne̥ Barnabe̥n' Ẽ̆yte, eδe Păuline̥ Merkūr","Εδέ κ̇ϳȣ́ανινε̰ Βαρνάβε̰νε̰ Έν̇ε̰τε, εδέ Πάβλ̇ινε̰ Με̰ρκȣ́ρ"]}],"f":[["Êjte",23],["Enjëte",171],["Ejtes",23]],"ety":{"text":"Based on the correspondence between e enjte (“Thursday”) and the descendants of Latin diēs Iovis, most likely artificially coined by Konstandin Kristoforidhi himself for his 1872 New Testament translation. Note how the form Ejtes in Rossi (1875) is not listed under Rossi (1866), page 266: “Giove” → “Jov, ɛot o hȣinìi rreiscme”. Mjeda (1932) harshly calls the attestation by Rossi a meaningless phantasy.","tpl":[{"k":29,"lang":"la","form":"diēs Iovis"}]},"rel":{"syn":["Zeus","Dhia"]}}],"evgjeni":[{"w":"Evgjeni","p":"PROPN","s":[{"g":"a female given name","t":12}]}],"fermele":[{"w":"fermele","p":"NOUN","s":[{"g":"a kind of ornamented jacket worn afore the torso"}],"f":[["fermelé",158],["fermele",3,3],["fermeleja",2,3],["fermele",13,3],["fermeletë",11,3],["fermele",1,3],["fermelenë",0,3],["fermele",8,3],["fermeletë",7,3],["fermeleje",18,3],["fermelesë",17,3],["fermeleve",10,3],["fermeleve",9,3],["fermeleje",16,3],["fermelesë",15,3],["fermelesh",6,3],["fermeleve",5,3]],"tpl":["sq-noun-f-të"],"ipa":[{"i":"/fɛɾmɛˈlɛ/"}],"ety":{"text":"Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish فرمله (fermele), فرمنه (fermene).","tpl":[]}}],"gacë":[{"w":"gacë","p":"NOUN","s":[{"g":"heat, hot ashes","t":12},{"g":"spark, sparkle","t":12}],"f":[["gaca",14],["gaca",4],["gacat",19],["gacë",3,3],["gaca",2,3],["gaca",13,3],["gacat",11,3],["gacë",1,3],["gacën",0,3],["gaca",8,3],["gacat",7,3],["gace",18,3],